      "id": 32,
      "title": "The Settling of the Church of Vharess",
      "type": "Organization",
      "categoryId": 4,
      "summary": "Vharess's church settled in the year 167 around the one institution dragonborn honor could not supply clan by clan: a school above the clans, where the standard of formation is public, the honor-rolls are held in neutral custody, and the first stone was deliberately left rough.",
      "body": "Of the churches so far, Tharnok's settled around a book and Khargun's around a road. Vharess's settled around a school, which the dragonborn would tell you is the same thing as a church, a fortress, and an argument, conducted daily, with meals. The temple-schools of Vharess are the oldest shared institution the dragonborn have, older than any treaty between the clans, and the clans agreed to them only after a century of agreeing about nothing else.\n\n## Before the church\n\nThrough [[The Uncounted Time]], every clan taught its own. Vharess gave the dragonborn their breath and their clans in the same gesture, and her teaching, as every clan kept it, was short: the template expects to be exceeded. What counted as exceeding, however, each clan decided at its own fires. One clan's youths were formed on arms and little else. Another held that breath discipline was the whole of the law. A third produced exquisite calligraphers whose honor-rolls were works of art and whose sword work was a clan embarrassment.\n\nThe arrangement had a flaw that proud peoples find fastest: comparison. Dragonborn honor is a public thing, kept on honor-rolls, and the rolls of neighboring clans described youths trained to standards nobody shared. Marriages between clans stalled over whose formation counted. Mercenary companies hiring across clan lines could not tell a finished education from a local opinion. Twice in the second century of the count, arguments over whose youths were properly formed ended in formal duels between clan champions, which settled the duels and nothing else.\n\n## The settling\n\nIn the year 167 of the First Age, the clan speakers did the thing dragonborn do when honor cannot settle a question: they built the answer where everyone could watch it.\n\nOn neutral ground between three clan-states, each clan laid one course of the foundation of a school that would belong to no clan: a temple-school of Vharess, where the standard of formation would be set in public, taught in public, and argued about in public ever after. The building still stands, and the Laying of the First Stone is still how the church dates itself, because of what the masons did with that stone. Every clan sent its best mason. When the moment came to lay the first stone, the masons set it down beside the foundation instead, and left it rough and unworked, where it stands to this day.\n\nThe doctrine of the church is in that stone, and every dragonborn child learns it on the first day: the template is given, and what is given is not finished. Perfection done to you is not improvement. A school that squared its first stone would be teaching that something in it was complete, and nothing in a temple-school of Vharess is complete, least of all the students.\n\n## What they took charge of\n\nEducation, above clan lines, which made the schools the first dragonborn institution that outranked a clan inside its own borders. A youth's formation is certified by the school, not the clan, and the certification is entered on the honor-rolls, which the schools now keep for all the clans in neutral custody, the way Tharnok's church keeps the Ledger. See [[The Settling of the Church of Tharnok]]. The curriculum is breath, arms, letters, craft, and the honor code, taught by teacher-priests whose whole theology is that self-improvement is worship and standing still is the only heresy the church prosecutes.\n\nThe manner of leaving is the school's signature. A student does not graduate. A student exceeds: before witnesses, in the teacher's own presence, the student must do one named thing better than the teacher who taught it to them, and the doing is recorded on the roll. The church is unsentimental about the reverse case. A teacher whose students never exceed them is retired with full honors and no new students, because a master unexceeded is a warning, not a glory.\n\nThe schools also conduct the dragonborn dead to Vharess, who carries them to [[Khavora, the Maelstrom Womb|Khavora]], and the funeral teaching is the school's teaching stated once more: a dragonborn who wakes finished has not been rewarded. He has been robbed. See [[Patrons]].\n\n## The unfinished building\n\nThe First School is the church's oddity and its sermon. Every class of students adds one course of stone to it, in the style of their own day, under orders to leave the course they add slightly, visibly improvable by the next. The building therefore reads like the honor-rolls made solid: a hundred and forty courses of masonry, each correcting the last, none of them final, creeping slowly taller on its hill between the clan-states. Pilgrims from other peoples visit it and ask when it will be done, and regard the laughter they get as rudeness. It is not rudeness. It is the catechism, answered correctly.\n\nOther temple-schools are plainer, and all of them keep the rule in small: a rough stone over the door, unworked, where a finished lintel would go.\n\n## The church today\n\nThere is a temple-school in every clan-state and a school-chapel in most places abroad where clan companies serve under contract, and the schools double as embassies, courts of honor, and the one address where a message for any clan can be left with certainty of reaching all of them. The honor-rolls they keep are consulted in disputes the way other peoples consult treaties, and companies hiring dragonborn read a school's roll the way they would read a bond.\n\nIt is, by some distance, the most respectable of the churches, which the dragonborn consider a working condition rather than a compliment. Where Khargun's priests are read and Tharnok's are audited, Vharess's are examined, perpetually, by their own students, in public, as a form of devotion. See [[The Settling of the Church of Khargun]]. The next church to settle belonged to a people who measure time in centuries and saw no reason at all to hurry. See [[The Settling of the Church of Aelith]].",
